# CareerGuide AI — frontend configuration
# Copy to `frontend/.env.local`. Defaults already point at the local backend,
# so the app works without this file during development.

# Base URL of the FastAPI backend, reachable from the browser.
#
# This value is also written into the site's Content-Security-Policy (see
# src/middleware.ts): the origin here is allowed in `connect-src` and `media-src`,
# which is what lets a chat reply be fetched and spoken audio be played. Change it
# and the policy changes with it — but change it and nothing else, and every screen
# still renders while every request is refused in the browser console. On a hosted
# deployment this must be the public https origin of the backend, and the backend's
# FRONTEND_URL must name this site back (docs §1, §2.2).
#
# A blank value counts as no value: `lib/api.ts`, `lib/site.ts` and `src/middleware.ts`
# all fall back to `http://localhost:8000` when the variable is unset *or* set-but-empty,
# because a variable created in Vercel with an empty box is otherwise a build that bakes
# in nothing and sends every API call to this site's own origin, where `/api/health` is a
# 404 page. On Vercel this must be an **Environment Variable**, not a **Secret** — a
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` secret is not available when the client bundle is built, so it silently
# ships the fallback instead.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:8000

# The address this app's OWN server code fetches from, when it runs inside a
# container or behind another hostname. Leave it unset on a laptop: `localhost`
# there means the machine everything is on, and the public address above is
# already right. Set it to the docker-network name (`http://api:8000`) in a
# compose stack, where `localhost` inside the web container is the web container.
# Not a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` name on purpose — it must never be inlined into the
# bundle, because a browser cannot resolve it. `docker-compose.yml` already
# supplies the right value, so this line is only for someone running the web
# image somewhere else.
# BACKEND_INTERNAL_URL=

# Contact form delivery (POST /api/contact). All three are needed before a
# message can actually be emailed; with any of them missing the endpoint answers
# 503 "not_configured" and the contact page says so on screen instead of
# pretending the message was sent. Leave these unset until a real mailbox exists.
# Values are secrets — put them in .env.local, never here and never in a commit.
RESEND_API_KEY=
CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=

# Clerk — the optional email sign-in. Nothing here is needed to use the app.
#
# The student's account is this browser; no email address or password is ever
# required. These keys only add the *choice* of opening the same work from a new
# device. The backend keeps a pointer to Clerk's user id and nothing else — no
# email, no name, no picture.
#
# This one is public by design and ships in the browser bundle. Its partner,
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY, is a server credential: set it in `backend/.env`, never here,
# and point Clerk's webhook at POST /api/webhooks/clerk using
# CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET there as well.
#
# Left blank, /sign-in, /sign-up and /account/enter say plainly that email sign-in
# is not switched on for this site, and no Clerk code runs on any route.
#
# Set it, and src/middleware.ts adds Clerk's own hosts to the Content-Security-
# Policy. Those hosts are the ones Clerk's documentation names, not ones observed in
# a working sign-in here — no Clerk project is attached to this deployment — so the
# first time email sign-in is switched on, complete a sign-in in a browser with the
# console open before trusting that line.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Razorpay — deliberately nothing here.
#
# There is no NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID in this app on purpose. The publishable
# key arrives from the backend inside `GET /api/payments/me` and the order
# response, so the browser never needs a build-time copy of it, and a value baked
# into a bundle at build time is a second place the same key has to be kept in
# step. Set RAZORPAY_KEY_ID in `backend/.env` and the pricing page lights up;
# leave it blank and the page disables its own Upgrade buttons and says why.
#
# The npm package `razorpay` is likewise not installed here: it is the *server*
# SDK, and its whole job is to call the Razorpay API with the key secret. A
# frontend copy of it would mean either shipping that secret to every visitor or
# carrying a dependency nothing can use. Instead `src/lib/checkout.ts` loads
# https://checkout.razorpay.com/v1/checkout.js on the click that asks for it, and
# `src/middleware.ts` allows that host in `script-src`, `frame-src` and
# `connect-src`, plus `payment=(self "https://checkout.razorpay.com")` in the
# Permissions-Policy. Those allowances are unconditional, and the reason is in
# that file: middleware cannot know at request time whether the backend has keys.
# If a checkout sheet ever opens blank, look for a blocked frame in the console
# before you look for a bug in the code.

# Analytics — Google Analytics 4 and Microsoft Clarity. Both are OFF unless a
# project id is named here, and even with one named they only load when the
# visitor has accepted the analytics category.
#
# These two values are public identifiers, not secrets: they ship inside the
# browser bundle and are visible to anyone who opens devtools. A measurement id
# is not a credential — it identifies which property numbers land in, and it
# cannot be used to read them. Keep every actual secret (the GA4 API key, a
# Clarity REST token, a Clerk secret key) on the server instead.
#
# With either set, `src/middleware.ts` adds that service's hosts to the
# Content-Security-Policy automatically. If a script is blocked in the console
# after you fill one of these in, restart the dev server — the policy is built
# per request from these values.
#
# What these two services never see: a student's screens, a parent's screens, the
# entry screens and the account screens. `components/analytics/Analytics.tsx`
# refuses to mount there, because Clarity's page replay would otherwise be
# recording the text a child types. See the privacy page's Cookies section.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=

# This site's own public origin, used for canonical URLs, the OpenGraph card URL
# and `/sitemap.xml`. Set it to the deployment's real URL — `https://` plus the
# host, no trailing slash — or every absolute link handed to a search engine and
# to WhatsApp points at the default below instead.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://careerguide-web.vercel.app

# Google Search Console domain verification. Paste the value Google shows under
# "Verification method → HTML tag" for the site, which is the string inside its
# `name="google-site-verification"` attribute. It is not a secret: it is in the
# page's HTML for anyone to read, and it grants nothing by itself.
#
# Left empty, no verification meta tag is emitted at all — which is different
# from claiming a half-configured one. After setting it, choose the URL-prefix or
# domain property in Search Console and submit
# `<your site>/sitemap.xml` there.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# School and counsellor portals — both of these are about *addresses on screen*, and
# neither of them is an access check. Permission comes from the server, from the
# membership row, on every request.
#
# The domain a portal's subdomain would hang off, e.g. `careerguide.localhost` for
# `green-school.careerguide.localhost`. The portal screens use it to say what a portal's
# address would be; when it is empty they print the `/school/dashboard?portal=<slug>`
# path instead and explain that this deployment has no portal subdomains configured.
# Keep it the same value as the backend's TENANT_BASE_DOMAIN, or a page will name an
# address the API does not recognise.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TENANT_BASE_DOMAIN=

# Hosts a portal's own logo may be drawn from. Comma-separated bare hostnames — no scheme,
# no path — and `*.` prefix is allowed for a whole domain. Whatever is listed here is also
# added to the page's Content-Security-Policy `img-src`, so this is the single list that
# decides both whether a logo URL is accepted and whether the browser is allowed to fetch
# it. Empty, which is the default, means no external logo is drawn anywhere: a portal's
# branding then shows its name and colours only.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOWED_LOGO_HOSTS=

# Who is answerable for this deployment — the identity the legal pages print.
#
# `/privacy`, `/contact`, `/terms` and `/about` all read one module,
# `src/lib/legal-entity.ts`, instead of carrying their own copy of these strings,
# so a real launch fills this block once and every page agrees. Left unset, those
# pages show sample values and print one short note beside them saying they are
# samples; set all five and that note disappears on its own, because there is then
# nothing left to warn about.
#
# These are not secrets and are not treated as such: all five are printed on pages
# anyone can open, which is why they are `NEXT_PUBLIC_` names. Anything actually
# secret — `RESEND_API_KEY` above, a Clerk secret key, a database password — does
# not belong in this block.
#
# Two fields are deliberately absent from this list. A grievance *officer*'s name
# gets no sample value, because inventing a person for a child's privacy policy is
# a fabrication nobody can be sued for; the row simply does not appear until
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_OFFICER names a real human. A phone number gets no sample
# either, because "call us" is a promise somebody has to staff — with it unset the
# contact page says the email above is the only line in.
#
# After changing any of these, restart the dev server and re-run `npm run build`:
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are compiled into the bundle, so a deployment that sets
# them in its dashboard must build AFTER setting them, not before.
#
# The trading name, and the name a court would accept if one ever comes here.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_COMPANY=CareerGuide
#
# The person who reads complaints, once there is one.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_OFFICER=
#
# The inbox a grievance or data question is addressed to.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_EMAIL=grievance@careerguide.com
#
# Where paper goes, and the city whose courts the terms point at. Keep the city
# inside the address, or the two pages will name different places.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_ADDRESS=123 Tech Park, New Delhi, India
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_CITY=New Delhi
#
# Optional. A number here is printed as reachable, so leave it empty until someone
# is actually rostered to answer it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_PHONE=
#
# Optional. The answer window printed beside the officer's details.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEGAL_REPLY_DAYS=30 days
